m.checkOut(5, "Paradise", 16); // Customer 5 "Leyton" -> "Paradise" in 16-10 = 6\nundergroundSystem.getAverageTime("Leyton", "Paradise"); // return 5.50000, (5 + 6) / 2 =','\n',char(10)),'1','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:12:17');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(57,replace('Design Underground system\n\nLink: https://leetcode.com/problems/design-underground-system/description/\n\nAn underground railway system is keeping track of customer travel times between different stations. They are using this data to calculate the average time it takes to travel from one station to another.\n\nImplement the UndergroundSystem class:\n\nvoid checkIn(int id, string stationName, int t)\nA customer with a card ID equal to id, checks in at the station stationName at time t.\nA customer can only be checked into one place at a time.\nvoid checkOut(int id, string stationName, int t)\nA customer with a card ID equal to id, checks out from the station stationName at time t.\ndouble getAverageTime(string startStation, string endStation)\nReturns the average time it takes to travel from startStation to endStation.\nThe average time is computed from all the previous traveling times from startStation to endStation that happened directly, meaning a check in at startStation followed by a check out from endStation.\nThe time it takes to travel from startStation to endStation may be different from the time it takes to travel from endStation to startStation.\nThere will be at least one customer that has traveled from startStation to endStation before getAverageTime is called.\nYou may assume all calls to the checkIn and checkOut methods are consistent. If a customer checks in at time t1 then checks out at time t2, then t1 < t2. All events happen in chronological order.','\n',char(10)),'1','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:14:28');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(58,replace('1274. Number of Ships in a Rectangle\n\nLink: https://leetcode.ca/2019-05-27-1274-Number-of-Ships-in-a-Rectangle/\nDescription\n(This problem is an interactive problem.)\n\nEach ship is located at an integer point on the sea represented by a cartesian plane, and each integer point may contain at most 1 ship.\nMathematics\n\n\nYou have a function Sea.hasShips(topRight, bottomLeft) which takes two points as arguments and returns true If there is at least one ship in the rectangle represented by the two points, including on the boundary.\n\nGiven two points: the top right and bottom left corners of a rectangle, return the number of ships present in that rectangle. It is guaranteed that there are at most 10 ships in that rectangle.\n\nSubmissions making more than 400 calls to hasShips will be judged Wrong Answer. Also, any solutions that attempt to circumvent the judge will result in disqualification.\n\n \n\n\nExample :\n\n\n\nInput: \nships = [[1,1],[2,2],[3,3],[5,5]], topRight = [4,4], bottomLeft = [0,0]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: From [0,0] to [4,4] we can count 3 ships within the range.\nExample 2:\nMaritime Transport\n\n\nInput: ans = [[1,1],[2,2],[3,3]], topRight = [1000,1000], bottomLeft = [0,0]\nOutput: 3\n\n \n\nConstraints:\n\nOn the input ships is only given to initialize the map internally. You must solve this problem "blindfolded". In other words, you must find the answer using the given hasShips API, without knowing the ships position.\n0 <= bottomLeft[0] <= topRight[0] <= 1000\n0 <= bottomLeft[1] <= topRight[1] <= 1000\ntopRight != bottomLeft','\n',char(10)),'1','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:16:20');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(59,replace('Yesterday I had a system design interview, and it was supposedly great. The system design question was:\n\n"""\nI was told to design a subsystem for a Bloomberg Terminal that gets data from the London Stock Exchange and displays it to users.\n\nFunctional Requirements\n- Users can subscribe to any kind of company stock they want to see real-time updates from.\n- Users can access historical data for a particular stock based on a time range.\n- Statistics and analytics are required as well.\n"""\n\nTips: I was able to ask the right questions, and the interviewer was actually very helpful in telling me the things I needed to know. He told me not to worry about the scale of the system. We went from functional requirements to core entities and then to high-level design. I started with a general client-server-database flow and improved it from there upon the interviewer''s request, since it contained some predicaments I needed to clarify and use. He also asked me about key technologies to address some issues regarding reads and writes. But overall, it was a fun interview, and I was able to arrive at a workable solution.','\n',char(10)),'3','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:18:31');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(60,replace('“Design Underground System”\n\nLeetCode #1396\nA design problem where passengers check in and out at stations with timestamps, and you compute average travel time between station pairs.\n(Often implemented with hash maps storing active check-ins and travel time aggregates.)','\n',char(10)),'2','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:20:49');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(61,replace('“Insert Delete GetRandom O(1)”\nLeetCode #380\nThis is the lottery one. The methods basically mirror:\n• insert(val)\n• remove(val)\n• getRandom()\nRequires average O(1) operations using a combo of a hash map plus array.','\n',char(10)),'2','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:21:19');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(62,replace('You will be given a grid and two points (a starting point and an ending point), and you will have a certain amount of fuel. The task is to check if it is possible to reach the ending point without running out of fuel. You can only travel horizontally and vertically.\n\nNote: This was my question but no multiple gas stations in the follow up','\n',char(10)),'2','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:23:29');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(63,'I''ve been asked more questions regarding A2SV experiences. I guess preparing in advance on your sections, which include A2SV and Eskalate, will help you more.','2','behavioral',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:24:14');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(64,replace('Invalid Transactions\n\nLink: https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-transactions/description/\n\nA transaction is possibly invalid if:\n\nthe amount exceeds $1000, or;\nif it occurs within (and including) 60 minutes of another transaction with the same name in a different city.\nYou are given an array of strings transaction where transactions[i] consists of comma-separated values representing the name, time (in minutes), amount, and city of the transaction.\n\nReturn a list of transactions that are possibly invalid. You may return the answer in any order.\n\n \n\nExample 1:\n\nInput: transactions = ["alice,20,800,mtv","alice,50,100,beijing"]\nOutput: ["alice,20,800,mtv","alice,50,100,beijing"]\nExplanation: The first transaction is invalid because the second transaction occurs within a difference of 60 minutes, have the same name and is in a different city. Similarly the second one is invalid too.\nExample 2:\n\nInput: transactions = ["alice,20,800,mtv","alice,50,1200,mtv"]\nOutput: ["alice,50,1200,mtv"]\nExample 3:\n\nInput: transactions = ["alice,20,800,mtv","bob,50,1200,mtv"]\nOutput: ["bob,50,1200,mtv"]\n \n\nConstraints:\n\ntransactions.length <= 1000\nEach transactions[i] takes the form "{name},{time},{amount},{city}"\nEach {name} and {city} consist of lowercase English letters, and have lengths between 1 and 10.\nEach {time} consist of digits, and represent an integer between 0 and 1000.\nEach {amount} consist of digits, and represent an integer between 0 and 2000.','\n',char(10)),'2','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:25:47');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(65,'custom index','3','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:26:16');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(66,replace('The Collatz Conjecture says if you take a positive integer N and repeatedly set either N=N/2 (if it''s even) or N=3N+1 (if it''s odd), N will eventually be 1.\n5 -> 16 -> 8 -> 4 -> 2 -> 1 (5 steps).\n\nGiven N, how many steps does it take to reach 1?\n\nTip: n/2, 3n+1 problem with multiple calls. He forced me to use iterative approach\n\nrelated link: https://leetcode.com/discuss/post/1504782/bloomberg-phone-interview-1042021-collat-gwfh/','\n',char(10)),'1','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:30:21');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(67,replace('Link: https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-intervals/\n\nYour trading platform aggregates multiple banks, each with its own trading hours. Customers can place trades anytime during the day, but trades can only be executed when at least one bank is open.\n\nGiven the operating hours of banks, design a function that can verify whether a customer can trade at any minute within the time of the given order.\n\nLet''s say:\norder1 = 15:00 - 20:00\nBanks:\n03:30 - 09:30    JP Morgan\n08:00 - 12:00    Bank of America\n10:00 - 15:00    National Bank\n14:00 - 18:30    HSBC\n16:00 - 20:00    Citi Bank\n19:00 - 23:00    Deutsche Bank\n\ncanTrade(banks, order1) = True\n\nThe follow-up part confused me a bit. Let''s say after finishing the schedule, we just return the final list. Now, if this was a class and we needed to add a new schedule, my idea is—instead of calling the original add function again (since it costs O(n log n))—I''d use the returned final list and search through it. That would only cost O(n). But to make it even faster, I''d use binary search, so it becomes O(log n).\n\nBasically, I''m trying to optimize the process by searching directly in the result list instead of re-running the whole add function.','\n',char(10)),'1','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:32:40');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(68,replace('You are given a string s consisting of any characters. Your task is to repeatedly remove any substring that contains three or more consecutive identical characters.\n\nAfter each removal, the remaining parts of the string are concatenated together, and the process continues until no such substring exists.\n\nReturn the final string after all possible removals.\n\n\nExample 1:\nInput: s = "aabbbbc"\nOutput: "aac"\n\nExample 2:\nInput: s = "aabbbbac"\nOutput: "c"\n\nFollow up: what if we can start at any index, instead of going form left to right? E.g: s = "aaabbbbaa" output: " "','\n',char(10)),'2','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:33:57');
INSERT INTO questions VALUES(69,replace('Design a note-taking app.\nShould be able to retrieve notes offline as well, \nit should be a mobile application.','\n',char(10)),'3','technical',X'',5139431786,'2026-08-09 13:35:22');
